      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 158,
      "outputTokens": 1156,
      "costUsd": 0.010483,
      "pronunciationText": "Now look inside the apartment savings. Say it earns six per cent a year, compounded monthly, which is zero point five per cent a month. So each month the balance is multiplied by one point zero zero five, and then the two hundred dollar deposit goes in. Month one starts with nothing, so there's no interest, and the balance is just two hundred. In month two, the two hundred earns one dollar, then the next deposit arrives, giving four hundred and one dollars. Month three gives six hundred and three dollars and one cent. It's one rule, used again and again. The next balance is one plus r, times this balance, plus M. The first deposit earns nothing in its own month, because it arrives at the end, after the interest."
    },
